2186512862 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512862 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512862 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186512567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512862 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ffa36a73-cb38-5a3e-b22f-94ae7972e4d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512867 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512867 #edb/ref 1069 "q-35003" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512867 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512867 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/06/06e792c5b74eda036b55ac3728122737da22c9824f89f982b50657ab07391269.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above? \n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=\\text{DNE}$\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=2$\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512867 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0821d791-2798-561e-8237-7b4aabdd155c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512867 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. As $x$ approaches $0$ from the left and right we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=0,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=1\\,.\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x),$ and hence ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. As $x$ approaches $1$ from the left and right we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=2,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\\,.\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x),$ and hence ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We see from the graph that \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\n$$\n\n We can't say that the limits are equal because we can't compare undefined values.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512873 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512873 #edb/ref 1069 "q-100106" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512873 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512873 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/51/51b119021be889ff440ec31d901187c3ee9072a507336a8e1a1854f729f2734f.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{-2f(x)}{3{x}^{2}}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512873 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "124cf999-fb4b-5e99-b208-917f67eaa2a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512873 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{-2f(x)}{3{x}^{2}}=\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 1}(-2f(x))}{{lim}_{x\\to 1}3{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe find from the graph that ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=6.$ Calculating the limits of the numerator and denominator separately, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}(-2f(x))=-2(6)=-12\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}3{x}^{2}=3(1)^{2}=3.\n$$\n\nTaking the quotient of these limits, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{-2f(x)}{3{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 1}(-2f(x))}{{lim}_{x\\to 1}3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-12}{3} \\\\  & =-4\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
